triage_response:
What you describe — hearing your name when no one is there, feeling people are watching or talking about you, and having jumbled thoughts — are signs that deserve prompt medical evaluation. These symptoms can reflect an acute change in thinking or perception and should be assessed by a clinician within a short timeframe so underlying causes (medical, medication- or substance-related, or a psychiatric condition) can be identified and addressed.

Arrange an urgent face-to-face evaluation with your primary care doctor, an urgent mental health clinic, or a psychiatrist within the next 24–48 hours. If you cannot get an outpatient appointment, go to the emergency department for immediate assessment.

Go to the emergency department or call emergency services right now if you:
- are thinking about harming yourself or others, or someone has told you they are worried you might do so;
- are unable to keep yourself safe or stay in a safe place;
- are severely confused, extremely agitated, or very hard to understand/talk to;
- are having very frightening or commanding hallucinations that tell you to do things;
- have stopped eating/drinking or caring for basic needs; or
- are intoxicated or have taken an unknown amount of medication/substances and feel very unwell.

If possible, have a trusted person stay with you or accompany you to evaluation and avoid driving or operating heavy machinery until you’ve been seen.

DISPOSITION: C -- Urgent: see a doctor within 24-48 h
